{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>When a man sends his animal to graze, etc.</b> It may be explained according to what they said regarding one who introduces [his animal] (Bava Kamma 56b): Rav said — one who stands his fellow's animal upon his fellow's standing grain, etc. — is liable. And they said there: even if it rose on its own, and even if it kicked, he is liable. And this is what the verse says — <b>when a man sends his animal to graze</b> — meaning, even if [the] grazing is not his. And the verse's saying <b>and sends his animal</b> means — even if he did not stand it there and it did not rise on its own and did not kick, but he merely sent it on its own and did not guard it from going, and it grazed by itself in another's field — if the animal is his, as the verse says 'his animal' by way of designation — he is liable [to pay from] the best, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The bracketed word [אֵין] ('is not') appears in the source; without it the phrase would read 'even if the grazing is his,' which reverses the meaning. The sense requires 'even if the grazing is not his,' so I translated accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:19.784790010Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}